Pauk *** READ-ME *** (July 1998)
____________________________________________________________

*** Installation ***
a) copy Pauk.opa >>> m:\app\*.*
b) mkdir m:\pau\
c) copy sys$help.opo >>> m:\opo\*.* (optional)
d) copy Pauk.hlp >>> m:\pau\*.* (optional)
e) copy tips.pau >>> m:\pau\*.*
f) install Pauk with <psion>-I
g) start "tips" and follow instructions

Pauk.opa may be installed on any drive. Help files must be installed 
on the internal drive (if help desired). It is strongly recommended
to keep all card files (*.pau) on the internal drive.
Have fun !
____________________________________________________________

It follows a copy of the online help text 
(available through the Psion help key).


*** Introduction ***
Pauk is a swotting utility. Or let's rather call it a flash card 
manager. You use it for any kind of data that you need to 
memorize, such as vocabulary, codes, acronyms, technical 
terms, etc. Whatever data can be considered as a question
/answer, or a term/translation pair is suitable for Pauk. 
Such a pair (and some more information) is stored as one
flash card (one Pauk record). 

And Pauk helps learning (see Learning guide) in an efficient 
way. Many bestselling books on human memory and learning 
have recommended flash cards again and again. And this is 
what Pauk is all about.

*** Cards & Levels ***
Each card has a left side (front), a right side (reverse) and a 
notes field. Left side, right side and notes may be swapped at
your discretion, individually or on many cards simultaneously.
Each field may contain any text up to 255 characters, in up to 
3 lines. Attributes provide additional means to find or sort 
cards. 

Each card is assigned a level (1-4), a new card is always on level 
1. <browse>, <list> and <learn> mode are all level-sensitive. See 
the keyboard guide on how to select your browse level. Individual 
card levels change when you <learn>, or may be set manually in 
the <set levels> dialog.

Pauk keeps track of when a card has changed (expressed as
'days gone'), be it by editing or learning. This may be used to 
find cards unseen for a while, or sort accordingly. The 'days 
gone' attribute may be reset individually, or for all cards together.

*** Learning ***
In <learn> mode one side of a card is hidden. Reading the other 
side, you guess what is hidden. Any key  unhides the missing 
text, and with the next key press you assign a new level to the 
current card:
#024 to promote by one level (right guess),
#025 to demote by one level (wrong guess),
<psion>#024 to set highest level,
<psion>#025 to reset lowest level,
<enter> to ignore.
Then the next card is presented for learning. Thus step by step 
you separate easy cards from more tricky ones. Next time you 
may select to learn on level 1 only, to review level 4 only, or to 
list them altogether. Learning can be from the left side, from 
the right side, or at random. 

*** Keyboard Guide ***
In browse mode:
   <1> to <4> selects a  level,
   <0> shows all cards,
   any character starts a quick search,
   <tab> edits the current card (edit mode)
   <shift-enter> edits the card attributes,
   <enter> moves to the next card,
   #024 (#025) moves to first (last) card, 
   #026 (#027) moves to next (previous) card,
   <space> creates a new card (enters edit mode),
   <del> deletes the current card,
   <esc> shows all levels found in stack.
 In list mode:
   any key lists the following cards (next screen),
   <esc> cancels list mode.
 In edit mode:
   <tab> selects the next field, or saves the (new) card,
   <enter> creates a new line,
   #024 (#025) moves to start (end) of line, 
   <del> and <shift-delete> work as usual,
   <esc> cancels edit mode.

*** Files & Memory ***
You may <open> one card file only at a time. Most commands 
only work when a card file is open. Pauk creates a backup-file 
upon <open>. <revert> overwrites the current file with its 
back-up. Files are saved automatically upon <new> or <open>. 
Upon <exit> Pauk saves the current file automatically.

There is no limit to the number of cards Pauk can handle (but 
your Psion's memory). However, we recommend to split 
piles of more than 150 - 250 cards into separate files. This 
will considerably improve speed of sort and find operations.

*** Tips ... ***
>> Learn : follow the same scheme each time, e.g. start on 
level 4 first, than continue on lower levels; or sort stack
and learn all levels simultaneously; or ... or consistently 
demote 'wrong' cards one level (or back to level 1); or ... <<

>> List View : is level sensitive and shows left sides, right 
sides and level of the cards. <<

>> Days gone : Used to identify cards not reviewed recently; 
like <level> or <type> this attribute may be reset in the 
attributes dialog at your discretion. <<

>> Find : the card last modified by pressing the down arrow.
Find any card with non-empty note field: enter '?' as search
string and search the notes. <<

>> Sort :  It's slow - but works. It is done on disk (not in RAM) 
and each time used the file size considerably increases. Yet, the 
file gets compressed again when you close it. <<

>> Old files : When working with a file that hasn't been used 
for a while consider to reset all 'levels' and 'days gone'.

>> Delete : want to delete multiple cards ? just use the 'move' 
option in the extract dialog, and delete the extracted file. <<

>> System calls: due to limitations in OPL Pauk cannot be exited
from the system screen (with <del>) when a dialog box is open.

*** Printing & Export ***
Pauk doesn't provide intrinsic print capabilities. Instead 
cards (or stacks) are <export>ed, than printed from within 'Word' 
or 'Sheet'. This provides access to the advanced formatting 
and previewing features of the Psion built-in applications and 
keeps Pauk's memory requirements low.

